---
import { css } from 'styled-system/css'
import Docs from '../../../layouts/Docs.astro'
import Callout from 'web-design-system/astro/marketing/Callout.astro'
import { Badge } from 'web-design-system/components'

const toc = [
  { label: 'What Shepherd is', href: '#what-shepherd-is' },
  { label: 'The mental model', href: '#mental-model' },
  { label: 'What works today', href: '#available' },
  { label: 'What is next', href: '#next' },
  { label: 'Where to start', href: '#where-to-start' },
]

const starts = [
  {
    title: 'Install and run',
    href: '/docs/users/install',
    body: 'The supported platforms, the development shell, and how to open your first project.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Mental model',
    href: '/docs/users/mental-model',
    body: 'What a project, task, worktree, terminal and review mean before you start clicking.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Preview and annotation',
    href: '/docs/users/preview',
    body: 'Point at an element, write the change, and send the annotation as structured context.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Troubleshooting',
    href: '/docs/users/troubleshooting',
    body: 'Agent discovery, authentication, stale terminals, worktree errors and local logs.',
  },
]
---

<Docs
  title="Overview"
  description="Shepherd is a local-first desktop workspace for several coding agents, their Git worktrees, terminals and reviews."
  toc={toc}
>
  <p>
    <strong>Shepherd</strong> runs the coding agents you already installed in your own repositories.
    It keeps several tasks visible at once, gives each one a real Git worktree, and puts the
    transcript, approvals, terminals, diff and preview in the same window. There is no account, no
    hosted sandbox and no Shepherd-owned model.
  </p>
  <p>
    The app is deliberately local. A Rust core owns every agent process, PTY and database write; the
    window is a renderer over that core. Quitting the window ends the work, and the next launch says
    which tasks were interrupted instead of pretending they kept running.
  </p>

  <h2 id="mental-model">The mental model</h2>
  <p>
    Most of Shepherd follows from four objects. The <a href="/docs/users/mental-model">mental model
    page</a> covers them in detail, including what happens on quit.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Project</strong> — a local directory you added, usually a Git repository.</li>
    <li><strong>Task</strong> — one conversation with one agent, in its own worktree, with state, transcript and review data.</li>
    <li><strong>Worktree</strong> — the isolated checkout an agent edits, so parallel tasks do not share a working tree.</li>
    <li><strong>Inspector</strong> — the always-on right region with Terminals, Preview, Review, Git and Files.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="available">What works today</h2>
  <div class={css({ display: 'grid', gridTemplateColumns: { base: '1fr', md: 'repeat(2, 1fr)' }, gap: '4' })}>
    <section class={css({ p: '5', borderRadius: 'lg', borderWidth: 'hairline', borderStyle: 'solid', borderColor: 'border', bg: 'surface.subtle' })}>
      <Badge tone="success" variant="subtle">available</Badge>
      <h3 class={css({ mt: '3' })}>The local workspace</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Several agents and projects in one window, with parallel tasks.</li>
        <li>Codex, OpenCode, Claude Code through ACP, and custom ACP agents.</li>
        <li>One Git worktree and branch per task, review, commit and squash-merge.</li>
        <li>Project-shared and task-specific terminals with splits and scrollback.</li>
        <li>Preview with element annotation and a screenshot fallback.</li>
        <li>Prompt queues, attachments, task search, notifications, themes and rebindable keys.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>
    <section class={css({ p: '5', borderRadius: 'lg', borderWidth: 'hairline', borderStyle: 'solid', borderColor: 'border', bg: 'surface.subtle' })}>
      <Badge tone="accent" variant="subtle">next</Badge>
      <h3 class={css({ mt: '3' })}>The agent pool and installers</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>A quota-aware pool that chooses the next healthy installed agent.</li>
        <li>Signed macOS and Linux release artifacts.</li>
        <li>A download path that resolves the newest release.</li>
      </ul>
      <p class={css({ fontSize: 'sm', color: 'fg.muted' })}>
        These are specified and being built, but they are not in the current build.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>

  <Callout tone="warning" title="The app is the source of truth">
    The <a href="/docs/users/agent-pool">agent pool</a> is not available yet. Windows support is
    planned with no date. If this site and the app disagree about what works, believe the app.
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="next">What is not Shepherd</h2>
  <p>
    Shepherd is not an agent and does not bundle one. It does not host a sandbox, run a daemon, sync
    your projects to an account, or replace your editor, Git client or deploy pipeline. It also has
    no telemetry: the only network requests are the ones your agents make, plus the loopback preview
    wrapper while the Preview tab is using it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="where-to-start">Where to start</h2>
  <div class={css({ display: 'grid', gridTemplateColumns: { base: '1fr', sm: 'repeat(2, 1fr)' }, gap: '4' })}>
    {
      starts.map((entry) => (
        <a
          href={entry.href}
          class={css({
            display: 'block',
            p: '5',
            borderRadius: 'lg',
            borderWidth: 'hairline',
            borderStyle: 'solid',
            borderColor: 'border',
            bg: 'surface.subtle',
            transitionProperty: 'border-color, background-color',
            transitionDuration: 'fast',
            _hover: { borderColor: 'border.strong', bg: 'surface.elevated' },
            _focusVisible: { outline: 'none', boxShadow: 'focus' },
          })}
        >
          <span class={css({ display: 'block', fontWeight: 'semibold', color: 'fg.default', mb: '1' })}>
            {entry.title}
          </span>
          <span class={css({ display: 'block', fontSize: 'sm', color: 'fg.muted', lineHeight: 'relaxed' })}>
            {entry.body}
          </span>
        </a>
      ))
    }
  </div>

  <Callout tone="note" title="Working on the code?">
    The <a href="/docs/contributors">contributor guide</a> covers the Rust core, adapter contracts,
    the annotation API, the quota-ledger design and the release pipeline.
  </Callout>
</Docs>
